comment:
The conditional logic order creates a potential issue. When `props.id === 'new'`, the first condition checks for `searchParams.sourceId != null` and if true, executes `actions.loadFeatureFlag()` and returns early. This means the second condition (`props.id === 'new' && values.featureFlag.id == null`) will never be evaluated when a source ID exists.

Consider restructuring these conditions to ensure both scenarios are properly handled:

```javascript
// First check if we're on the new page with no existing feature flag ID
if (props.id === 'new' && values.featureFlag.id == null) {
    // Only load if there's a sourceId, otherwise skip loading
    if (searchParams.sourceId != null) {
        actions.loadFeatureFlag()
    }
    return
}

// Otherwise load for existing feature flags
if (props.id) {
    actions.loadFeatureFlag()
}
```

This ensures the intended behavior for both cases: loading when there's a source ID to duplicate from, and skipping the load when creating a brand new flag with no source ID.

